  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale\nThe Erd\u0151s discrepancy problem seeks to minimize maximum auto-correlation. The approach combines:\n   a) Deterministic initial patterns to seed search space\n   b) Stochastic exploration through mutation\n   c) Global guidance via kernel density estimation\n   d) Local refinement via gradient descent\nBottlenecks addressed: Local optima avoidance, balance between exploration/exploitation, and ensuring strict feasibility. New in this plan are adaptive annealing schedules, multi-stage objective decomposition, and analytical gradient components.\n\n2. Mathematical specification\n   - Representation: Step function h(x) on [0,2] sampled at n_points, 0\u2264h[i]\u22641, sum(h)=n_points/2\n   - Objective: C_5 = max_k \u222bh(x)(1-h(x+k))dx computed via full correlation\n   - Support: n_window = 2*n_points - 1 (length of correlation)\n   - Algorithm: Divided into phases:\n     Phase1: Pattern-initiated restarts with structured mutations\n     Phase2: Simulated annealing with adaptive temperature\n     Phase3: Kernel-assisted gradient descent with block density estimation\n   - Local search uses: Smoothed correlation gradient\n   - Repair: Differential constraints with clipping ensured by projection\n   - Solver: Manual gradient assembly with penalty terms for constraints\n\n3. Implementation procedure\n   a) Initialization:\n      - Use provided parent if available (n=500)\n      - Create diverse initial population including:\n        1. Block functions (2,4,8,... blocks)\n        2. Random patterns\n        3. Structured synths (sine waves, pulses)\n      - All enforced as step functions\n\n   b) Evolutionary phase:\n      - Temporal phase allocation: 55% exploration, 40% refinement, 5% restarts\n      - Mutation operators: Statistical, Deterministic, Adaptive\n      - Selection: Tournament selection with cooling schedule\n      - External archive: Maintenance must avoid incest\n\n   c) Gradient-based refinement:\n      - Form assembly: Manual creation using masked penalties\n      - L-BFGS-B with automatic scaling\n      - Trust region strategy for numerical stability\n\n   d) Statistics-driven phase:\n      - Kernel density estimation from top solutions\n      - Substitute gradient calculations\n      - Stochastic search directions\n\n   e) Repair mechanism:\n      - Separate constraint handling: \n        $\\text{Clip}(0,1) + \\text{Projection}(\\text{AllZero}(\\delta))$\n      - Repair operates first on solutions sourcing in infeasibility\n\n   f) Phase transition decision: \n      - Criteria: CPU time, score improvement rate\n      - Early termination options\n      - Fallback on stagnation\n\n4. Search choices\n   a) Enumerated variations:\n      1. Pattern generation strategies (block, rhythmic, burst)\n      2. Mutation types: \n         - Global Gaussian (temporal focus)\n         - Local Block (block-based)\n         - Differential (template-inversion)\n      3. Temperature schedules:\n         - Linear decay\n         - Exponential\n         - Dynamic (problem-dependent)\n\n   b) Parameter settings:\n      - Cooling factor: Start with 0.95, reduce by noise factor\n      - Annealing steps: Coupled with population size\n      - Kernel bandwidth: Auto-selected from |solution basin|\n      - Block pattern executions: Historical adaptive\n\n5. Computation budget\n   - Core operations: \n     4-phase plan with 5-level budget breakdown\n     Each stage: Prioritize constraint satisfaction\n     Major components: Correlation cost (n^2), projection (n_points)\n   - Time estimate: 60/80/70% validation: Use analytical formulas for correlation breakdown\n   - Deadline handling: Internal clock checks at beginning/mid/end\n   - Fallback: Provide abort-ready fallback at beginning/mid/end\n\n6. Validation and return contract\n   a) Post-optimization checks:\n      Random sample projection ensuring feasibility\n      Re-check values via multiple calculation paths\n      Correlation re-computation with stripping of batches\n   b) Adherence test:\n      Uses: Scoring delivers better correlation\n      Algorithm progress: Tracks max improvement rate\n   c) Return format:\n      h_values: Must sum to 0.5 w/ dx=2/n\n      c5_bound: Computed via correlation\n      n_points: Exact point count",
